From 87405c198854860b65dcff6f1368f2dd92d7ae2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian MacDonald <ian@netstatz.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 18:27:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 038/102] fix(server): make parallel download resume durable
 and keep progress on local failures

Review follow-ups on the parallel Range download path.

Power-loss durability. The journal may only claim bytes that are on the
device, and it must be on the device before any out-of-order byte can
land:
- the .partial is opened once per attempt and synced (fdatasync; fsync
  on macOS; _commit on Windows) before the attempt's first journal write,
  before every prefix advance, and before the success-path journal
  removal;
- journal writes go through a synced tmp file and a durable rename
  (rename + parent directory fsync on POSIX, MoveFileEx with
  MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH on Windows);
- normalisation syncs the truncated partial before it removes the
  journal.
The journal is therefore durable before the first range request is sent,
and it only disappears after a synced complete file or a synced
truncation. A partial with interior holes can never exist on disk
without its journal, so the no-journal 416/HEAD "already complete" path
only ever sees a sequential single-stream partial or a synced complete
parallel one; it is left unchanged. The ordering is argued rather than
tested: power loss cannot be simulated portably from a unit test. The
test does assert that the journal exists on disk before the server sees
any range request.

Local setup failures (opening the partial, the journal write, multi or
easy handle setup, curl_multi_perform, the final flush) now return
can_resume with the partial normalised, instead of letting the retry
loop delete a valid prefix.

download_rate_limit and download_connections are re-checked before each
attempt; a cap set mid-download moves the remaining attempts to a single
stream.

A 416 on a ranged request keeps the journaled prefix and falls back to a
single stream, which re-checks the remote size, instead of taking the
permanent-4xx path that deletes the partial.

The Range probe honours g_download_cancelled and the progress callback,
so cancelling no longer waits for a slow probe.

